<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alauddin Riayat Shah II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1564">1564</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alauddin Riayat Shah II was the first sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>, a son of the last sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>, Mahmud Shah. After the Portuguese took Malacca in 1511, he founded the Johor Sultanate on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor-river">Johor River</a> around 1528 and continued the struggle against Portugal. He was captured during an Acehnese attack in 1564 and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alauddin Riayat Shah was the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-shah-of-malacca">Mahmud Shah of Malacca</a>. Because the new sultanate grew out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>, the sultans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> counted themselves its successors, descended from its rulers and continuing its numbering, which is why he is the second of his name. He married Princess Kesuma Dewi of Pahang, a daughter of Mansur Shah of Pahang.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After his father's death he set up his first capital in 1529 at Pekan Tua in Hujung Tanah, 11 km upriver from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kota-tinggi">Kota Tinggi</a>, with a river fort, Kota Kara, downstream. In 1535 about 400 Portuguese troops under Estêvão da Gama attacked. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malays">Malays</a> withstood the first bombardment and counter-attacked, but were scattered by Portuguese crossfire, and the fort was captured and burned. The sultan withdrew up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor-river">Johor River</a> to Sayong Pinang, then returned and rebuilt Pekan Tua. After Malays killed Paulo da Gama and about 30 other Portuguese, Estêvão da Gama attacked again, and a peace treaty followed. In 1540 the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor-lama">Johor Lama</a>, nearer the river mouth.</p><p>The rising Aceh Sultanate on the northern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> was a second threat. In 1539 a fleet of 160 ships and 12,000 soldiers, Acehnese with Malaccan Malays, Malabaris, Gujaratis and Turks, attacked Aru, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>'s vassal on the east coast of Sumatra. In 1540 the sultan, aided by his allies Perak and Siak, retook Aru at the Battle of Sungai Paneh, leaving only 14 Acehnese ships afloat and thousands of Acehnese dead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1564 Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-al-kahar">Alauddin al-Kahar</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> defeated Aru, expelled the Johoreans and attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor-lama">Johor Lama</a> from there. The fort and town were levelled and the sultan was carried off to Aceh, where he died of sickness. He received the posthumous title Marhum Syahid di Acheh and was succeeded by his son, Muzaffar Shah II.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alauddin Riayat Shah II of Johor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>One of his sons, Muzaffar Shah, went on to found the sultanate of Perak; another, <b>Alauddin Riayat Shah II</b>, established a new court on the Johor River at the southern tip of the Malay Peninsula.</blockquote><a href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Muzaffar Shah was invited to rule the north of the peninsula as Sultan of Perak, and <b>Alauddin Riayat Shah II</b> founded the Sultanate of Johor.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=5.57,95.34&amp;year=1554" aria-label="Aceh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Alauddin Riayat Shah II"><image href="/maps/globes/g09000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5454 4524h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5454 4524h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=5.57,95.34&amp;year=1554">What was here in 1554? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
